WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.269
-Our next guest is an
Emmy-nominated sports journalist

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:04.938
and lead sideline reporter
for "Fox NFL Sunday."

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:06.105
Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:06.105 --> 00:00:08.174
our friend Erin Andrews,
everybody!

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.042
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:25.191 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:28.127
Welcome back!
I am so happy you're here.

00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:31.064
I mean, anyone who watches
football knows that on Sunday

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:33.733
you were in terrible weather
in Cleveland, Ohio.

00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:35.401
And now look at you.
It's like you --

00:00:35.401 --> 00:00:36.703
Had no idea.

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:38.871
-You know,
a ton of undergarments

00:00:38.871 --> 00:00:40.406
that suck you in.

00:00:40.406 --> 00:00:42.575
And the baseball hat's gone.
And there we go. Yeah.

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:44.377
-It was a very --
It was terrible weather,

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:46.078
but it was
a very exciting game and --

00:00:46.078 --> 00:00:48.013
-Let's be honest.
It wasn't that bad of weather.

00:00:48.013 --> 00:00:50.850
I mean, it was on-and-off rain.
I love a weather report.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.019
What would you do
if you weren't doing this?

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:54.787
-You would do the weather?
-I would love to work

00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:56.890
for the Weather Channel
or Fox Weather.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:58.457
Yeah.
I would love to do that.

00:00:58.457 --> 00:01:00.560
I love miles-per-hour
wind gusts.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.128
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:03.863
I love, like, rain throughout.
That wasn't that funny.

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:06.132
But I love, like, you know,
the rain throughout the game.

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:07.666
-It wasn't funny.
It was shocking.

00:01:07.666 --> 00:01:09.402
-Oh.
-I don't think anybody's

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:10.803
ever heard someone be like,

00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:13.073
"I mean, who doesn't love
miles-per-hour wind gusts?"

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:14.907
-Right.
17-to-18-mile-per-hour winds

00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:16.276
we were dealing with yesterday.

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:18.010
-I will say
17-to-18-mile-per-hour wind --

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:20.546
When people say that
on the news, I always wonder,

00:01:20.546 --> 00:01:23.516
"Now what am I supposed to do
with that information?"

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.784
Like, have you ever heard --
At what mile per hour

00:01:25.784 --> 00:01:27.686
are you like,
"Well, I'm scrapping my plans"?

00:01:27.686 --> 00:01:29.588
-Well, yesterday 'cause
I had to put a hat on

00:01:29.588 --> 00:01:31.858
because, you know, you needed
the hair to be good to go.

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:34.127
-So at what mile per hour
do you go with hat?

00:01:34.127 --> 00:01:36.863
-So, 17 to 18.
I thought, did it affect --

00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:38.564
Listen.
We're paying attention.

00:01:38.564 --> 00:01:40.066
-I know. Yes.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:42.334
-You know, they missed the field
goal and all that stuff.

00:01:42.334 --> 00:01:43.836
But the weather
wasn't that crazy.

00:01:43.836 --> 00:01:45.571
-Gotcha.
-But it wasn't lovely.

00:01:45.571 --> 00:01:47.506
But it's that time of year.
It's football, baby.

00:01:47.506 --> 00:01:49.341
-You love it, right?
-Steelers.

00:01:49.341 --> 00:01:52.045
-Do -- Steelers are fine.

00:01:52.045 --> 00:01:53.913
-They come to LA next week.
-They do come to LA.

00:01:53.913 --> 00:01:55.915
And I'll tell you this.
I don't know who's gonna win.

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:58.451
But I know there will be
more Steelers fans in LA than --

00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:00.486
-Alright. Relax.
-I'm just telling you.

00:02:00.486 --> 00:02:02.455
-Have you gone to SoFi yet?
-I haven't gone to SoFi.

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:03.890
-Oh, yeah. It's cool.
-Is it cool?

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.091
The problem is there's just --

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:06.992
You know, LA sports fans
are a little different.

00:02:06.992 --> 00:02:08.394
Like, the Steelers fans
bring it.

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:10.596
-Okay. Well, they do.
Hey. One of my best --

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:13.433
We have -- This was nothing
that I talked about
with your producers.

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:16.602
One of my favorite
Steelers games

00:02:16.602 --> 00:02:19.606
was Dallas at Pittsburgh.

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:22.008
It was Dak and Zeke's,
like, rookie season.

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:23.910
I remember two minutes left.
-You can stop.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.346
-But you lost.
The towels were going.

00:02:26.346 --> 00:02:28.615
All the lint was flying.
It was awesome. Crazy scene.

00:02:28.615 --> 00:02:30.283
-By the way, you don't
ever have to tell me

00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:32.818
the ending of a Steelers game.
I have a --

00:02:32.818 --> 00:02:35.854
'Cause we went down
and scored on one of those --

00:02:35.854 --> 00:02:38.591
Roethlisberger was gonna
fake spike. And then touchdown.

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:41.727
You know what? That is a very
famous game in my household.

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:42.995
-Why?
-Because that is the first time

00:02:42.995 --> 00:02:46.732
my wife said,
"I don't like you after these."

00:02:46.732 --> 00:02:50.068
-So, my dad --
massive Red Sox fan.

00:02:50.068 --> 00:02:53.673
And I guess my parents'
relationship got a little rocky.

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:56.342
They're still together.
Love them. Yay! Clap for that.

00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:57.843
[ Applause ]

00:02:57.843 --> 00:02:59.245
But I guess --

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:01.447
You can figure out which
World Series moment it was.

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:03.583
I was a, like, young baby.

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:06.452
My dad took baby powder
and threw it across the room.

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:08.687
And my mom's like,
"You cannot behave like this."

00:03:08.687 --> 00:03:10.089
-Oh, my God.
-Do you have a moment

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:11.824
in your house?
Was it that one?

00:03:11.824 --> 00:03:13.626
-Yes, but here's --
Well, that's --

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:15.595
So, you're talking about '86,
for sure.

00:03:15.595 --> 00:03:17.363
So, the one --
the bad one in our house,

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:19.798
which was 2003,
the Aaron Boone home run.

00:03:19.798 --> 00:03:21.567
Now, again, the next year,

00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:23.836
the Red Sox win
and everything's hunky-dory.

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:25.871
-Yes.
-But my mom is only a sports fan

00:03:25.871 --> 00:03:28.474
because of my dad
and her two boys, right?

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:31.010
And so she's become
a huge sports fan

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.444
and she cares
more than any of us.

00:03:32.444 --> 00:03:34.747
And when Aaron Boone
hit the home run,

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:36.849
she stood up and --
according to my dad -- said,

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:39.952
"This is all the fault of you
and those mother [bleep] boys."

00:03:39.952 --> 00:03:41.654
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.654 --> 00:03:44.123
-Of course it is.
It is your fault.

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:46.559
-That's a mind-boggling
sentence to say, though.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:49.528
-Well. Traumatizing.
How's the PTSD from that one?

00:03:49.528 --> 00:03:51.163
-As with everything
in our family,

00:03:51.163 --> 00:03:52.832
we pretend
like it never happened.

00:03:52.832 --> 00:03:55.301
-Yeah, exactly.
-Now, you have a very --

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:57.270
Obviously,
I'm not breaking news here

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.639
that one of the biggest fans...
-Wow.

00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:04.076
-Usually a fan does not affect
an NFL season like this.

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:05.745
-Right.
-But Taylor Swift, who --

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:07.079
credit where credit's due --

00:04:07.079 --> 00:04:09.715
in August,
you and your podcast host...

00:04:09.715 --> 00:04:11.383
-Yep. Charissa Thompson. Yep.

00:04:11.383 --> 00:04:12.952
-You and Charissa
basically said to --

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:14.687
You basically threw it out
to the universe,

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:16.422
and you thought Travis
should go on a date.

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:18.057
-Have you met him?
-Yes. He's wonderful.

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:19.559
-He's great. Right?
-He's wonderful.

00:04:19.559 --> 00:04:21.093
She's wonderful.
I totally understand

00:04:21.093 --> 00:04:22.795
why you guys thought
this would be a good match.

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:24.496
-Yeah.
-But even more exciting,

00:04:24.496 --> 00:04:26.299
you have an apparel line,

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:30.402
and this is sort of NFL gear
for the female fan.

00:04:30.402 --> 00:04:32.238
-Yeah.
-And she wore it to a game.

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:33.773
Now --
-She wore it last Thursday.

00:04:33.773 --> 00:04:35.308
Yeah. It's our --

00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:36.408
[ Cheers and applause ]
Thank you. Yeah.

00:04:36.408 --> 00:04:38.144
This was big, man,
and, you know --

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:41.080
-I mean, I got to imagine if
you've got an apparel brand...

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.082
-If you have anything
and she puts it on.

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:45.585
I mean, if you own a Kleenex
company and she wipes her --

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:48.888
it's like -- Oh, my God!
Yes. This was a huge moment.

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:51.657
And, yeah, in August,
Travis was very open

00:04:51.657 --> 00:04:53.559
about not getting to meet her.

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:55.862
And so we were just being really
candid because we love him.

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:57.729
We're good friends of his.
And we just were like,

00:04:57.729 --> 00:04:59.998
"Taylor, date this guy.
I mean, he's good-looking."

00:04:59.998 --> 00:05:02.068
And I can say that
'cause my husband loves him

00:05:02.068 --> 00:05:03.769
and they've met before.

00:05:03.769 --> 00:05:06.138
Got to be really careful where
you throw the "good-lookings."

00:05:06.138 --> 00:05:07.706
-Yeah.
-But, yeah, he's just

00:05:07.706 --> 00:05:09.308
such a great, great guy.

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:11.344
And so we threw it out there.
They got together.

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:13.179
Of course, we're getting
all the credit now

00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:14.480
on the "Calm Down" podcast.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.049
We hope to officiate
the wedding.

00:05:17.049 --> 00:05:19.485
We hope to be there when
he proposes, all the things.

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:22.220
But, yeah, she put the --
she put my jacket on.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:23.555
-Are you getting calls

00:05:23.555 --> 00:05:26.091
from the warehouse being like,
"We need more staff!"?

00:05:26.091 --> 00:05:27.392
Like, what happens?

00:05:27.392 --> 00:05:29.161
-Yes. Yeah. Well, we restocked.

00:05:29.161 --> 00:05:31.197
As soon as she wore it,
I saw it.

00:05:31.197 --> 00:05:33.132
I started screaming
and freaking out

00:05:33.132 --> 00:05:35.501
and singing all
my favorite Swiftie songs.

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:38.571
And then, yeah, we restocked
and we sold out.

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:40.706
And thank you, thank you,
Taylor Swift!

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:44.510
And now starting today,
we have more up on fanatics.com.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:48.947
And your wife, I heard,
has the same impact

00:05:48.947 --> 00:05:50.649
when she wears team apparel.
-She does. Yeah.

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:52.651
-So you know we're sending you
some Pittsburgh gear.

00:05:52.651 --> 00:05:54.286
-That'd be great.
We're gonna get her out there.

00:05:54.286 --> 00:05:55.988
-Flying off the shelves. Yeah.

00:05:55.988 --> 00:05:57.456
-It'd be hard to know
what the spike

00:05:57.456 --> 00:05:59.458
is attributable to, you know?

00:05:59.458 --> 00:06:01.093
Is it Taylor or is it Alexi?
But, you know...

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:02.762
-Yeah. Exactly.
Well, we'll see. We'll compare.

00:06:02.762 --> 00:06:04.163
I'll let you know.
-You and Charissa --

00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:05.798
You mentioned. I do want
to hammer home again.

00:06:05.798 --> 00:06:07.500
It's really wonderful.
You guys are old friends.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.602
And "Calm Down" podcast
is a delight.

00:06:09.602 --> 00:06:12.037
And it does other things
other than match-make.

00:06:12.037 --> 00:06:14.740
It's a wonderful --
It's a wonderful podcast

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:16.509
where two friends talk about
what they're experts in.

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:17.909
And, hey, thanks so much
for being here.

00:06:17.909 --> 00:06:19.445
-Thanks for having me.
-It's always such a pleasure.

00:06:19.445 --> 00:06:20.913
Erin Andrews, everybody.

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:23.582
You can catch her every week
on "Fox NFL Sunday."

00:06:23.582 --> 00:06:26.485
And "Calm Down" is available
wherever you get your podcasts.

00:06:26.485 --> 00:06:29.055
Stick around. We'll be back
with music from Ryan Beatty.

